[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m ### Step 3: Use the perimeter
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m The perimeter of the polygon is 20. The perimeter is the sum of all sides:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m \sum_{i=1}^{11} A_iA_{i+1} = 20
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m We know that:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m - $ A_1A_2 = a_2 $
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m - $ A_1A_3 = a_3 $
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m - ...
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m - $ A_1A_{11} = a_{11} $
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m So the perimeter is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m a_2 + a_3 + \ldots + a_{11} = 20
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m But from the earlier result, for each $ i $, $ a_i b_i = 26 $, so for each triangle $ A_iA_1A_{i+1} $, the product of the two sides is 26.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m So:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m a_i b_i = 26 \quad \text{for } i = 2, 3, \ldots, 10
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m $$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m So we have 9 such pairs, and the 11th side $ A_1A_{11} $ is $ a_{11} $, which is not part of the triangle $ A_{10}A_1A_{11} $, so it's not part of the product $ a_i b_i $.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m So the total perimeter is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=13888)[0m $$
